# deploy scripts

The prod **WRITE** steps of the deploy skill, each as a single argv so an
auto-mode classifier judges one named script with validated arguments instead of
a fresh multi-line remote command every deploy. Anything that writes to prod
belongs here, not inline in `SKILL.md`.

| Script | Deploy step | Writes | Success marker |
|---|---|---|---|
| `prod-deploy-lock.sh` | Step 3.5 (acquire), Step 7 (release / mark-failed) | the lock directory `{{ prod_remote_project_dir }}/.deploy-lock` on the box | `LOCK_ACQUIRED` / `LOCK_RELEASED` / `LOCK_MARKED_FAILED` |
| `prod-deploy-modules.sh` | Step 2, every deploy | pulls `{{ odoo_version }}`, updates modules, restarts the service | `DEPLOY_EXIT=0` |
{% if use_queue_job %}| `prod-requeue-jobs.sh` | Step 6, after a clean deploy | `queue.job` enqueued/started → pending | `REQUEUED <n>` |
{% endif %}

Every script here takes the host alias as its first argument (`prod`{% if prod_local_ssh_host %}, `prod-local`{% endif %}{% if test_ssh_hosts %} or a test alias{% endif %}),
refuses any other host, and **exits non-zero when its marker is missing**, so a
silent no-op cannot read as success.

Call them with the host spelled out — that is the form the permission rules
match:

```bash
bash "${{ project_dir_var }}/.claude/skills/deploy/scripts/prod-deploy-lock.sh" prod acquire <PR> --wait 1800
bash "${{ project_dir_var }}/.claude/skills/deploy/scripts/prod-deploy-modules.sh" prod -u <modules>
bash "${{ project_dir_var }}/.claude/skills/deploy/scripts/prod-deploy-lock.sh" prod release <PR>
{% if use_queue_job %}bash "${{ project_dir_var }}/.claude/skills/deploy/scripts/prod-requeue-jobs.sh" prod
{% endif %}```

## Permissions

Keep the call form byte-identical to whatever `.claude/settings.local.json`
authorizes — an extra flag or a missing host argument does not match the rule
and falls back to the classifier, mid-deploy, with the start announcement
already out.

Two rule shapes, and the difference matters:

- **`permissions.allow`** entries are argument-exact strings, so a
  `Bash(bash "…/prod-deploy-modules.sh" prod -u sale)` rule covers exactly that
  module list and nothing else. Fine for the requeue script (its arguments never
  change), useless for the deploy script.
- **`autoMode.allow`** entries are prose the classifier judges, so one entry can
  authorize *this script with any valid module list* — that is the entry the
  deploy script needs, and the script validates its own arguments (host,
  `-u`/`-i`, `^[a-z0-9_,]+$` module list) so the prose can describe a closed set.
  The lock script needs the same shape: its PR number changes every deploy, and
  it validates host, action (`acquire|status|release|mark-failed`), a numeric
  PR, a numeric `--wait` and `--force`.

**Claude cannot write its own permission file** — by any tool, and the refusal
is not negotiable. Compose the entry, hand it to the user, and let them paste
it. Do that *before* the first deploy of a new module, not after the deploy
stalls halfway.

## The deploy lock

Several developers deploy from their own clones of this project, and each
learns that another deploy is running only from a chat message they may not
have read yet. `prod-deploy-lock.sh` is the machine check: a directory
`{{ prod_remote_project_dir }}/.deploy-lock` on the box, created with `mkdir`
(atomic — two deployers racing for it cannot both win), holding one line
`holder=<gh login> pr=<n> since=<UTC> state=deploying|failed`.

- **Taken before the merge** (Step 3.5), because a merged PR lands on prod
  with the next `git pull`, whoever runs it; **released after the result is
  posted** (Step 7). `prod-deploy-modules.sh` refuses to run without it
  (`DEPLOY_LOCK_MISSING`, `DEPLOY_EXIT=3`), so a deploy that skipped the
  skill's Step 3.5 stops before the pull.
- `acquire <PR> --wait 1800` retries every 5 minutes for up to 30, one short
  ssh call per attempt (the box drops long sessions; an unanswered attempt is
  repeated, three in a row give `SSH_UNREACHABLE`). Still held after the wait →
  `LOCK_HELD holder=… since=… age=…m`, exit 3: the skill stops and reports who
  holds it. **Nothing releases another holder's lock automatically** — a
  Step-2b deploy legitimately holds it with the service stopped for minutes.
  `release --force` exists for the human who has checked with the holder, and
  runs only on their explicit command.
- **A failed deploy keeps the lock**, `state=failed` (`mark-failed <PR>`): prod
  may be half-migrated and the next deploy must not land on top of it. The
  acquire loop does not wait on a failed lock; the person who repairs prod
  releases it.
- Test boxes carry their own lock, same script, the alias as host.
- The lock lives in this checkout on the box and is in `.gitignore`.

## Project write steps

A project that needs another write before the invariant check (refreshing a
reporting table whose cron will not fire inside the check window, for example)
adds its own script here and documents it as a numbered step in the project's
`SKILL.md`, the same way `deploy-checks/scripts/invariant_local.py` extends the
read-only check. Same contract: one argv, host as the first argument, a printed
success marker, non-zero exit when the marker is missing.
